ex:Def_def_aus_1992_1285 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(Australia 1992) An area, incorporating all living and non-living components, th..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Australia ;
    ex:country "Australia" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_aus_1992_1285_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Ross_Penny_Ross ;
    skos:note "(Australia 1992) An area, incorporating all living and non-living components, that is dominated by trees having usually a single stem and a mature or potentially mature stand height exceeding 2 metres, and with existing or potential projected crown cover of overstorey strata about equal to or greater than 20 percent. This definition includes both native and plantation regardless of age. (From: \"Ross Penny\"Ross.Penny@nre.vic.gov.au the definition of forest used by the National Forest Inventory (and based on the 1992 Australian National Forest Policy Statement). We include all of the 'tree-forming' mallees (a plant of the genus Eucalyptus) in our definition of forest. From: Roger.Hnatiuk@brs.gov.au. It is also sufficiently broad to encompass areas of trees that are sometimes described as woodlands. http://www.brs.gov.au/nfi/forestinfo/summary.html  From:Jim.Stevenson@affa.gov.au This definition includes Australia's diverse native forests and plantations, regardless of age. It is also sufficiently broad to encompass areas of trees that are sometimes described as woodlands. http://www.australiansforanimals.org.au/afanewsjan.html and Karol.Andrzejewski@affa.gov.au NFI (1992) definition of Forest (from National Forest Policy Statement)"@en .

ex:Def_def_aus_1992_1285_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "2.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "2.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_aus_1992_1578 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(Australia 1992) An area, incorporating all living and non-living components, th..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Australia ;
    ex:country "Australia" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_aus_1992_1578_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Ross_Penny_Ross ;
    skos:note "(Australia 1992) An area, incorporating all living and non-living components, that is dominated by trees having usually a single stem and a mature or potentially mature stand height exceeding 2 metres, and with existing or potential projected crown cover of overstorey strata about equal to or greater than 20 percent. This definition includes both native and plantation regardless of age. (From: \"Ross Penny\"Ross.Penny@nre.vic.gov.au the definition of forest used by the National Forest Inventory (and based on the 1992 Australian National Forest Policy Statement). We include all of the 'tree-forming' mallees (a plant of the genus Eucalyptus) in our definition of forest. From: Roger.Hnatiuk@brs.gov.au. It is also sufficiently broad to encompass areas of trees that are sometimes described as woodlands. http://www.brs.gov.au/nfi/forestinfo/summary.html  From:Jim.Stevenson@affa.gov.au This definition includes Australia's diverse native forests and plantations, regardless of age. It is also sufficiently broad to encompass areas of trees that are sometimes described as woodlands. http://www.australiansforanimals.org.au/afanewsjan.html and Karol.Andrzejewski@affa.gov.au NFI (1992) definition of Forest (from National Forest Policy Statement)"@en .

