ex:Def_def_eur_2009_83 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(EU Copenicus) The forest type product allows to get as close as possible to the..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2009_83_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    skos:note "(EU Copenicus) The forest type product allows to get as close as possible to the FAO forest definition. In its original (20m) resolution it consists of two products: 1) a dominant leaf type product that has a MMU of 0.5 ha, as well as a 10% tree cover density threshold applied, and 2) a support layer that maps, based on the dominant leaf type product, trees under agricultural use and in urban context (derived from CLC and imperviousness 2009 data). For the final 100m product trees under agricultural use and urban context from the support layer are removed http://land.copernicus.eu/pan-european/high-resolution-layers/forests"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2009_83_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_2009_844 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(EU Copenicus) The forest type product allows to get as close as possible to the..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2009_844_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    skos:note "(EU Copenicus) The forest type product allows to get as close as possible to the FAO forest definition. In its original (20m) resolution it consists of two products: 1) a dominant leaf type product that has a MMU of 0.5 ha, as well as a 10% tree cover density threshold applied, and 2) a support layer that maps, based on the dominant leaf type product, trees under agricultural use and in urban context (derived from CLC and imperviousness 2009 data). For the final 100m product trees under agricultural use and urban context from the support layer are removed http://land.copernicus.eu/pan-european/high-resolution-layers/forests"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2009_844_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_2010_55 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(EU 2010) Continuously forested areas are defined as areas where trees have reac..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2010_55_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    skos:note "(EU 2010) Continuously forested areas are defined as areas where trees have reached, or can reach, at least heights of five metres, making up a crown cover of more than 30 percent….They would normally include forest, forest plantations and other tree plantations such as palm oil. Short rotation coppice [the practice of repeatedly cutting young tree stems down] may qualify if it fulfils the height and canopy cover criteria. Phillips, Leigh. 2010. Palm oil plantations are now 'forests,' says EU. EUObserver.com. http://euobserver.com/9/29410."@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2010_55_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "30.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "30.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_2010_56 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(EU 2010). Continuously forested areas are defined as areas where trees have rea..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2010_56_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    skos:note "(EU 2010). Continuously forested areas are defined as areas where trees have reached, or can reach, at least heights of 5 metres, making up a crown cover of more than 30%. They would normally include natural forest, forest plantations and other tree plantations such as oil palm. This means, for example, that a change from forest to oil palm plantation would not per se constitute a breach of the criterion (para 4.2.3, draft communication).” http://www.tropenbos.org/file.php/4/forests-dec21-final-web.pdf"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2010_56_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "30.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "30.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float .

