ex:Def_def_usa_1998_1187 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(USA-STATE-Michigan) TREE: It might seem silly, at first, to define a tree. Ever..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_USA ;
    ex:country "USA" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_usa_1998_1187_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_USA-STATE-Michigan ;
    skos:note "(USA-STATE-Michigan) TREE: It might seem silly, at first, to define a tree. Everyone knows what a tree is . . . don't they? The difference between and tree and shrub can be defined without difficulty. A tree is \"a woody perennial plant, typically large and with a well-defined stem or stems carrying a more or less definite crown --- note sometimes defined as attaining a minimum diameter of 5 inches and a minimum height of 15 feet at maturity, with no branches within 3 feet of the ground\" [Society of American Foresters, 1998]. The problem is that some species can grow as trees or shrubs depending on climate and site conditions. A good example is black cherry. Throughout most of its range, it grows as a tree. But put on a poor site or along the northern edge of its range and it grows as a shrub. Species that are obviously trees further south will sometimes occur only as shrubs in the far north. http://mff.dsisd.net/PDF/0-IntroStuff/4-Glossary.pdf"@en .

ex:Def_def_usa_1998_1187_criteria ex:minHeight "15.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "4.572"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_usa_1998_1523 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(USA-STATE-Missouri 1998) Although we do not have \"official\" definitions, we com..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_USA ;
    ex:country "USA" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_USA-STATE-Missouri ;
    skos:note "(USA-STATE-Missouri 1998) Although we do not have \"official\" definitions, we commonly use the accepted SAF definitions for these terms. See Dictionary of Forestry; John A. Helms, editor; published 1998 by the Sociecy of American Foresters. \"Mike Hoffmann\" hoffmm@mail.conservation.state.mo.us"@en .

