    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_usa_2001_1304_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_USA-FED-DA-FS ;
    skos:note "(USA-FED-DA-FS 2001) Land that is at least 10 percent stocked by forest trees of any size, or land formerly having such tree cover, and is not currently developed for a nonforest use. The minimum area for classification as forest land is one acre. Roadside, stream-side, and shelterbelt strips of timber must have a crown width at least 120 feet wide to qualify as forest land. Unimproved roads and trails, streams and other bodies of water, or natural clearings in forested areas shall be classified as forest, if less than 120 feet in width or an acre in size. Grazed woodlands, reverting fields, and pastures that are not actively maintained are included if the above qualifications are satisfied. (Also see definitions of nonforest land, idle farmland and improved/maintained pasture.) Forest land is divided into timberland, reserved timberland and woodland. SRS FIA Southern Research Station Field Guide; Version 1.5; January 8, 2001, Updated March 23, 2001; Based upon FIA National Core Field Guide, Version 1.5, January, 2001 and FIA National Manual v1.4"@en .

